Component AssemblyReturn to the Unity Editor and wait for the compilation
progress bar to complete.Open your persistent runtime initialization
scene (or your active working scene).Right-click the Hierarchy window 
and choose Create Empty. Name this GameObject [AI_MCP_Bridge].Drag and
drop the script from Assets/Scripts/Bridge/UnityBridgeAdapter.cs 
directly onto this new GameObject.In the Inspector panel, look at the
component fields. Ensure logEventsOnly is checked (true) and leave 
enablePosting unchecked (false) for initial safe local testing.2. Local 
Node.js Runtime VerificationOn your top engine menu bar, navigate to
Window > AI Game Developer > Start Server.Open the Unity Console panel.
Look for an initialization acknowledgment from the Node server process.
Troubleshooting Check: If you see a launcher crash error containing 
"Node command not found", your local development machine requires a 
global Node.js runtime installation. (Install Node LTS from your system
terminal or official site to resolve).3. Telemetry and State Change 
TestingPress the Play button in the Unity Editor.Trigger an action in
your game that forces the GameModeManager.OnStatusChanged event to fire 
(such as updating tree growth parameters or switching environmental 
seasons).Monitor your Unity console. You should see a perfectly 
serialized printout matching the structured BridgeEventData JSON layout
containing your real-time treeState telemetry metrics.4. Flip the Active
Bridge FlagStop the Unity playback state.Select your [AI_MCP_Bridge] 
GameObject.In the Inspector, check the box to switch Enable Posting to 
true.Your GitHub Copilot Chat interface using Claude 3.5 Sonnet inside
VS Code will now have real-time bidirectional access to modify and read 
your running scene context.📦 Push Infrastructure Changes to Remote 
